# Concrete AES-256 circuit for the proposed simulator

This scratch component implements the public AES-256 function as an explicit
Boolean circuit. It includes all key expansion, fourteen rounds and reversible
cleanup. No value-dependent table lookup, memory oracle, AES opcode or unpriced
AES macro is used. It does not implement a primitive reduction, substitute for
the external classical PRP challenge interface, or qualify production.

The circuit follows [FIPS 197 (2023)](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/197/final),
sections 3.4, 4 and 5.1–5.2. Its byte tests also use NIST's
[AES-256 example](https://csrc.nist.gov/CSRC/media/Projects/Cryptographic-Standards-and-Guidelines/documents/examples/AES_Core256.pdf).
The exact PDFs and extracted standard text are retained in `references/`.
This is correspondence to the specified AES function. It is not a proof about
RustCrypto's native implementation, acceleration, compiler, allocator or stack.

The selected Rust `crates/iroha_zkp_vole/src/prg.rs` takes a 32-byte seed for the
256-bit profile. It encrypts 16-byte blocks assembled from the actual IV, adding
the public block counter to its low little-endian 32-bit word, and the tweak to
its high little-endian 32-bit word. Both additions wrap separately. The middle
eight bytes are preserved. Those counter/tweak additions, prefix truncation,
partial-byte clearing and the complete surrounding generator invocation are
outside this AES kernel; the B1/V0 machine must emit and price them explicitly.

## Interface and construction

Input wires encode `key[32] || plaintext[16]`. Bytes retain their original order;
bit zero of each byte is its low coefficient. The 384 input wires, 128 output
wires and 201,587 work wires must be pairwise disjoint. The output starts with
any value, and work starts zero. The emitted map is

```
|key, plaintext, z, 0_work>
    -> |key, plaintext, z XOR AES256(key, plaintext), 0_work>.
```

The low-bit polynomial representation uses `x^8+x^4+x^3+x+1`. Multiplication
emits all 64 pair products, XORs coefficients, and reduces degrees 14 through 8
with four fixed taps. Squaring is the explicit binary linear map on the eight
basis monomials. Inversion uses the exponent chain

```
1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 6 -> 12 -> 15 -> 30 -> 60 -> 120 -> 240 -> 252 -> 254.
```

There are four multiplications and seven squarings per S-box. The chain maps
zero to zero and every nonzero element to its inverse. The FIPS affine map,
including its `0x63` constant, completes the S-box. The implementation contains
no S-box table; the tests retain an independent literal 256-byte table.

State bytes use the FIPS column-major layout. ShiftRows is fixed wire routing.
MixColumns uses fixed `xtime` and XOR operations. The 256-bit key expands into
60 four-byte words with RotWord, SubWord and the seven applicable Rcon bytes.
The initial AddRoundKey is followed by thirteen full rounds and the final
SubBytes/ShiftRows/AddRoundKey round. SubWord in the expansion contributes 52
S-boxes; the cipher contributes 224, for 276 total.

The generic Boolean IR allocates a fresh zero target per intermediate value.
It computes all nodes, CNOT-copies the ciphertext into the supplied target,
then reverses the node sequence. No history is discarded early. This proof is
for a clean output-XOR circuit, so invoking the same clean map again reverses
it even with an arbitrary output. All gates have their literal phase-free
classical reversible action; equality on every basis state extends by
linearity to coherent and externally entangled inputs.

## Exact component resources

The source-shaped IR has 70,656 AND nodes, 129,820 XOR nodes and 1,111 NOT nodes.
Its 201,587 derived wires are retained until the reverse traversal. It has no
registered subkernels or additional clean work. Using the existing B6
convention of five elementary gates per Toffoli, the complete clean AES map is:

| Kind | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| X | 2,222 |
| CNOT | 521,630 |
| Toffoli | 141,312 |
| Elementary total | 1,230,412 |
| Base XOR-oracle calls | 0 |
| Clean work bits | 201,587 |
| Additional input/output bits | 384 / 128 |

These counts are exact for this emitted construction, not optimal AES bounds,
native instruction counts, physical qubits, timing, or a complete simulator
resource vector. Every use by the surrounding machine must charge this full
kernel cost, its own routing and intermediate targets, and any outer
compute/copy/uncompute duplication. Reusing work is justified only because
each call returns it to zero. A whole-machine reverse traversal must retain
the appropriate original input/log versions; this stateless kernel does not
resolve that separate lifetime obligation.

The root's exploratory component and report checks pass 23 cases, including all 256 S-box
values and their emitted clean maps, independent finite-field and matrix
checks, the key schedule, five NIST known answers, full relocated AES
cleanup/inversion, an emitted gate census and source/refusal controls. A separate
local reviewer checked the arithmetic and counts and passed 13 independent
controls, including all sixty NIST Appendix A.3 key words and a packed-basis
interpreter of the complete emitted circuit. These
are finite checks plus a construction argument, not external cryptographic
review. TODO: capture the reviewed final component and integrate it into the
full source-bound B1/V0 machine before claiming complete machine coverage.

